/* English. The keys are the source strings, except for these. */

/* The "?" next to each display setting: what it does to latency and clarity. */
"help.mode" = "Extend adds a display made at the chosen resolution, with nothing scaled, and is the sharpest choice. Mirror shows one of the Mac's displays on the tablet, fitted to its proportions, with text a little less crisp. The latency is the same.";
"help.resolution" = "Fewer pixels mean less work for the encoder and less data on the network, so less latency but softer text. The tablet's native resolution is the sharpest and the heaviest, over Wi-Fi above all. Automatic uses the native resolution over the cable and, over Wi-Fi, the one that suits your tablet model, at most 1920 pixels wide on the densest screens.";
"help.fps" = "At 60 Hz movement is smooth and each change leaves within 17 ms. At 30 Hz there are half the frames, so with the same bandwidth each can be sharper, but movement is less smooth and a change may wait up to 33 ms.";
"help.codec" = "Automatic picks H.264 (the quickest to encode and decode) and, for Sharpest, HEVC (a more faithful picture with the same bandwidth, for a few milliseconds more). Either way the tablet takes the first it decodes in hardware.";
"help.priority" = "Lowest latency sends light frames that arrive sooner, with text a little softer when much changes. Sharpest allows more bandwidth and larger frames, for a more faithful picture that takes longer to arrive. Balanced sits between them. Either way the bandwidth goes down by itself when the network falls behind and back up when it is free again. Advanced changes any of this one setting at a time.";
"help.connection" = "Automatic uses the cable when the tablet is on it and Wi-Fi otherwise. The cable gives the lowest and steadiest latency, about 10–20 ms, and charges the tablet meanwhile. Wi-Fi leaves you free to move even with the cable on, with a latency that depends on the network and a sharpness that drops when the bandwidth is not enough.";

/* Advanced. */
"help.bitrate" = "The most the stream may use. Automatic takes it from Optimize for and the resolution. The bandwidth still goes down by itself when frames arrive late, and back up to this.";
"help.frame" = "How large one frame may be, against the average: a large frame (much changed at once, a keyframe) takes longer to send. 1× keeps every frame quick to arrive but blurs big changes for a moment; 8× keeps them sharp but they arrive later.";
"help.packets" = "How the frames are cut. Large packets cost the tablet fewer decryptions (on a slow tablet, several milliseconds a frame); over Wi-Fi they travel in IP fragments. Small ones fit one Ethernet frame each, for networks that drop fragments. Automatic uses large ones and turns to small ones if no frame arrives.";
"help.keyframes" = "A keyframe stands alone and is large. One comes whenever the tablet lost something; these come besides, in case. Rarer ones mean fewer peaks of latency.";
"help.stats" = "Measured by the tablet, with the clocks of both matched: from the Mac capturing a frame to the tablet having it decoded (median and 90th percentile), and its parts. The screen adds one or two refreshes to it.";
